   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.1",
    "topic_title": "Types of Chemical Bonds",
    "stem": "A student compares four single bonds: C–H, C–O, C–F, and O–F. Using periodic trends in electronegativity, which statement correctly identifies the most polar of these bonds and the atom that carries the partial negative charge (δ−) in it?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "O–F is the most polar, with δ− on F, because O and F are the two most electronegative elements in the set.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This choice judges polarity by how electronegative the atoms are rather than by the difference between them. O and F are adjacent in period 2, so their electronegativities are close and the O–F bond is only weakly polar. Fix: Subtract the two atoms' electronegativities, or compare how far apart they sit in the period, before ranking bond polarity."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "C–F is the most polar, with δ− on C, because the more electronegative F pulls so strongly that it leaves C holding extra electron density.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This reverses the dipole direction. Electronegativity measures an atom's pull on shared electrons, so the electron density shifts toward F. That makes F partially negative and leaves C partially positive. Fix: Ask which atom pulls harder on the shared pair, and place δ− on that atom."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "C–F is the most polar, with δ− on F, because F is more electronegative than C and C and F are farthest apart in period 2, giving the largest electronegativity difference.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Electronegativity increases from C to O to F across period 2. C and F are farthest apart, so they have the largest difference and the most unequal sharing. The shared electrons are pulled toward the more electronegative atom, F, which therefore carries δ−. This bond has the most ionic character of the four."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "All four bonds are equally nonpolar covalent with no ionic character, because each bond joins two nonmetals.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats ionic and covalent as two separate boxes and lets the nonmetal–nonmetal shortcut override the electronegativity comparison. Bonds between nonmetals with unequal electronegativities are polar covalent, and every polar bond has some ionic character. These four bonds differ in polarity. Fix: State the electronegativity comparison for each bond before naming its type, rather than classifying by metal or nonmetal identity alone."
     }
    ]
   },
