    "stem": "The electronegativity values of H, Cl, and Br are 2.2, 3.2, and 3.0, respectively. Which statement correctly describes the H–Cl and H–Br bonds in HCl and HBr?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Both bonds are nonpolar covalent because both bonded atoms are nonmetals; neither bond has a partial negative end.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Trap: nonmetal–nonmetal means nonpolar. Nonmetal pairs generally form covalent bonds, but unequal electronegativities produce unequal electron sharing and partial charges in these bonds. Fix: Compare the two electronegativity values before deciding whether a covalent bond is polar or nonpolar."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Both bonds are polar covalent, and H–Cl is more polar, but H carries δ− in each bond because H has the lower electronegativity.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Trap: reversed partial charges. The lower-electronegativity atom attracts shared electrons less strongly, so H is partially positive, not partially negative, in both bonds. Fix: Assign δ− to the atom with the higher electronegativity and δ+ to the other atom."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Cl and Br are more electronegative than H, so both bonds are polar covalent, with δ− on the halogen. H–Cl is more polar, and both bonds have some ionic character.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The electronegativity differences are 1.0 for H–Cl and 0.8 for H–Br, so H–Cl is more polar. Each halogen attracts the shared electrons more strongly than H does, making the halogen partially negative. Unequal sharing gives polar covalent bonds some ionic character."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Both bonds are ionic because any electronegativity difference makes a bond ionic rather than covalent.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Trap: treating any unequal electron attraction as ionic bonding. Unequal electronegativities can produce polar covalent bonds; having some ionic character does not require classifying a bond as ionic. Fix: Compare the size of the electronegativity difference and distinguish unequal sharing from predominantly ionic bonding."
     }
    ]
   },
