   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.1",
    "topic_title": "Types of Chemical Bonds",
    "stem": "A student compares four single bonds between second-period atoms: C–F, N–F, O–F, and F–F. Electronegativity increases from left to right across a period. Which statement correctly identifies the most polar of these bonds and describes how its bonding electrons are distributed?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "C–F is the most polar; the shared electrons are pulled toward F, so F carries the partial negative charge.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Across period 2, electronegativity rises C < N < O < F, so carbon differs most from fluorine. The largest electronegativity difference gives the largest bond dipole. Fluorine, the more electronegative atom, pulls the shared pair closer and carries δ−, while carbon carries δ+."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "O–F is the most polar; it joins the two most electronegative atoms, and the shared electrons are pulled toward F.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap ranks polarity by how electronegative the atoms are rather than by the difference between them. O and F are close in electronegativity, so their pull on the shared pair is nearly balanced, and O–F is the least polar of the heteroatomic bonds listed. Fix: Compute or estimate the electronegativity difference for each pair before ranking bond polarity."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "C–F is the most polar; the shared electrons are pulled toward C, so C carries the partial negative charge.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The bond is chosen correctly, but the partial charges are reversed. Carbon is less electronegative than fluorine, so the shared pair spends more time near F, leaving C partially positive. Fix: Ask which atom is more electronegative, and place δ− on that atom."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "C–F is the most polar; the electrons are fully transferred to F, so it is an ionic bond rather than a polar covalent bond.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap treats ionic and covalent as two separate boxes, so a large difference is read as complete transfer. C and F are both nonmetals that share electrons unequally, so C–F is polar covalent, with partial charges rather than full ionic charges. A large difference only means more ionic character. Fix: Place the bond on the continuum, and ask whether the electrons are shared unequally or truly transferred."
     }
    ]
   },
